{"timestamp":"2025-09-09T10:51:53.657Z","level":"debug","message":"Claude output","data":{"line":"{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01WPPhHuhYQDbzqv4ngJvHYX\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"     1→# Contributing to Efflux Web\\n     2→\\n     3→Thank you for your interest in contributing to Efflux Web! This guide will help you get started.\\n     4→\\n     5→## Development Setup\\n     6→\\n     7→### Prerequisites\\n     8→\\n     9→- Node.js 18+\\n    10→- npm or yarn\\n    11→- Git\\n    12→- A Supabase account (for testing)\\n    13→\\n    14→### Local Development\\n    15→\\n    16→1. **Fork and Clone**\\n    17→   ```bash\\n    18→   git clone https://github.com/your-username/efflux-web.git\\n    19→   cd efflux-web\\n    20→   ```\\n    21→\\n    22→2. **Install Dependencies**\\n    23→   ```bash\\n    24→   npm install\\n    25→   ```\\n    26→\\n    27→3. **Environment Setup**\\n    28→   ```bash\\n    29→   cp .env.example .env.local\\n    30→   # Edit .env.local with your Supabase credentials\\n    31→   ```\\n    32→\\n    33→4. **Run Development Server**\\n    34→   ```bash\\n    35→   npm run dev\\n    36→   ```\\n    37→\\n    38→## Project Structure\\n    39→\\n    40→```\\n    41→efflux-web/\\n    42→├── app/                    # Next.js App Router pages\\n    43→│   ├── (auth)/            # Authentication pages\\n    44→│   ├── (dashboard)/       # Main application pages\\n    45→│   └── api/               # API routes\\n    46→├── components/            # React components\\n    47→│   ├── ui/                # Base UI components\\n    48→│   ├── chat/              # Chat-related components\\n    49→│   └── models/            # Model management components\\n    50→├── lib/                   # Core libraries\\n    51→│   ├── ai/                # AI provider integrations\\n    52→│   ├── crypto/            # Encryption utilities\\n    53→│   ├── supabase/          # Supabase client\\n    54→│   └── stores/            # State management\\n    55→├── types/                 # TypeScript type definitions\\n    56→└── supabase/             # Database schema and migrations\\n    57→```\\n    58→\\n    59→## Contributing Guidelines\\n    60→\\n    61→### Code Style\\n    62→\\n    63→We use ESLint and Prettier for code formatting:\\n    64→\\n    65→```bash\\n    66→# Check formatting\\n    67→npm run lint\\n    68→\\n    69→# Auto-fix formatting\\n    70→npm run lint:fix\\n    71→```\\n    72→\\n    73→### Git Workflow\\n    74→\\n    75→1. **Create a Feature Branch**\\n    76→   ```bash\\n    77→   git checkout -b feature/your-feature-name\\n    78→   ```\\n    79→\\n    80→2. **Make Changes**\\n    81→   - Keep commits small and focused\\n    82→   - Write clear commit messages\\n    83→   - Follow conventional commit format\\n    84→\\n    85→3. **Test Your Changes**\\n    86→   ```bash\\n    87→   npm run build\\n    88→   npm run dev\\n    89→   ```\\n    90→\\n    91→4. **Submit Pull Request**\\n    92→   - Fill out the PR template\\n    93→   - Include screenshots for UI changes\\n    94→   - Link related issues\\n    95→\\n    96→### Commit Message Format\\n    97→\\n    98→We follow [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/):\\n    99→\\n   100→```\\n   101→type(scope): description\\n   102→\\n   103→[optional body]\\n   104→\\n   105→[optional footer]\\n   106→```\\n   107→\\n   108→Examples:\\n   109→- `feat(chat): add streaming response support`\\n   110→- `fix(auth): resolve login redirect issue`\\n   111→- `docs(readme): update installation instructions`\\n   112→\\n   113→Types:\\n   114→- `feat`: New feature\\n   115→- `fix`: Bug fix\\n   116→- `docs`: Documentation changes\\n   117→- `style`: Code style changes\\n   118→- `refactor`: Code refactoring\\n   119→- `test`: Adding tests\\n   120→- `chore`: Build process or auxiliary tool changes\\n   121→\\n   122→## Adding New Features\\n   123→\\n   124→### Adding AI Providers\\n   125→\\n   126→1. **Create Provider Class**\\n   127→   ```typescript\\n   128→   // lib/ai/providers/new-provider.ts\\n   129→   import { BaseAIProvider } from '../base'\\n   130→   \\n   131→   export class NewProvider extends BaseAIProvider {\\n   132→     // Implement required methods\\n   133→   }\\n   134→   ```\\n   135→\\n   136→2. **Register Provider**\\n   137→   ```typescript\\n   138→   // lib/ai/manager.ts\\n   139→   import { NewProvider } from './providers/new-provider'\\n   140→   \\n   141→   // Add to AIManager\\n   142→   ```\\n   143→\\n   144→3. **Update Settings UI**\\n   145→   ```typescript\\n   146→   // app/(dashboard)/settings/page.tsx\\n   147→   // Add new provider tab and configuration\\n   148→   ```\\n   149→\\n   150→### Adding UI Components\\n   151→\\n   152→1. **Create Base Component**\\n   153→   ```typescript\\n   154→   // components/ui/new-component.tsx\\n   155→   import * as React from 'react'\\n   156→   import { cn } from '@/lib/utils'\\n   157→   \\n   158→   const NewComponent = React.forwardRef<...>(...) => (\\n   159→     // Component implementation\\n   160→   )\\n   161→   ```\\n   162→\\n   163→2. **Export from Index**\\n   164→   ```typescript\\n   165→   // components/ui/index.ts\\n   166→   export * from './new-component'\\n   167→   ```\\n   168→\\n   169→3. **Add to Storybook** (when available)\\n   170→\\n   171→## Testing Guidelines\\n   172→\\n   173→### Manual Testing\\n   174→\\n   175→Before submitting a PR, test:\\n   176→\\n   177→1. **Authentication Flow**\\n   178→   - Registration\\n   179→   - Login/logout\\n   180→   - OAuth (if configured)\\n   181→\\n   182→2. **Core Features**\\n   183→   - Chat functionality\\n   184→   - Model switching\\n   185→   - API key management\\n   186→   - Conversation management\\n   187→\\n   188→3. **Error Handling**\\n   189→   - Invalid API keys\\n   190→   - Network errors\\n   191→   - Rate limiting\\n   192→\\n   193→### Adding Tests (Future)\\n   194→\\n   195→We plan to add:\\n   196→- Unit tests with Jest/Vitest\\n   197→- Integration tests with Playwright\\n   198→- Component tests with Testing Library\\n   199→\\n   200→## Code Review Process\\n   201→\\n   202→### For Contributors\\n   203→\\n   204→1. **Self-Review**\\n   205→   - Test your changes thoroughly\\n   206→   - Check for console errors\\n   207→   - Verify responsive design\\n   208→   - Review your own code\\n   209→\\n   210→2. **PR Description**\\n   211→   - Explain what and why\\n   212→   - Include before/after screenshots\\n   213→   - List breaking changes\\n   214→   - Reference related issues\\n   215→\\n   216→### For Reviewers\\n   217→\\n   218→1. **Code Quality**\\n   219→   - TypeScript usage\\n   220→   - Performance implications\\n   221→   - Security considerations\\n   222→   - Accessibility\\n   223→\\n   224→2. **Testing**\\n   225→   - Manual testing\\n   226→   - Edge cases\\n   227→   - Error scenarios\\n   228→\\n   229→## Security Guidelines\\n   230→\\n   231→### API Key Handling\\n   232→\\n   233→- Never log API keys\\n   234→- Always encrypt before storage\\n   235→- Use secure headers for transmission\\n   236→- Implement proper validation\\n   237→\\n   238→### Database Security\\n   239→\\n   240→- Use Row Level Security (RLS)\\n   241→- Validate all inputs\\n   242→- Implement proper authorization\\n   243→- Use parameterized queries\\n   244→\\n   245→### Frontend Security\\n   246→\\n   247→- Sanitize user inputs\\n   248→- Implement CSP headers\\n   249→- Use HTTPS in production\\n   250→- Validate all API responses\\n   251→\\n   252→## Performance Guidelines\\n   253→\\n   254→### Frontend Performance\\n   255→\\n   256→- Use React.memo for expensive components\\n   257→- Implement proper loading states\\n   258→- Optimize images and assets\\n   259→- Minimize bundle size\\n   260→\\n   261→### API Performance\\n   262→\\n   263→- Implement proper caching\\n   264→- Use streaming for long responses\\n   265→- Optimize database queries\\n   266→- Consider rate limiting\\n   267→\\n   268→## Common Issues\\n   269→\\n   270→### Development Issues\\n   271→\\n   272→1. **Supabase Connection**\\n   273→   ```bash\\n   274→   # Check environment variables\\n   275→   echo $NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL\\n   276→   ```\\n   277→\\n   278→2. **Build Errors**\\n   279→   ```bash\\n   280→   # Clear Next.js cache\\n   281→   rm -rf .next\\n   282→   npm run build\\n   283→   ```\\n   284→\\n   285→3. **Type Errors**\\n   286→   ```bash\\n   287→   # Regenerate types\\n   288→   npm run db:types\\n   289→   ```\\n   290→\\n   291→## Getting Help\\n   292→\\n   293→### Community Support\\n   294→\\n   295→- **Discord**: [Join our Discord](https://discord.gg/efflux)\\n   296→- **GitHub Issues**: [Report bugs or request features](https://github.com/your-username/efflux-web/issues)\\n   297→- **Email**: support@efflux.ai\\n   298→\\n   299→### Documentation\\n   300→\\n   301→- **API Documentation**: [Supabase Docs](https://supabase.com/docs)\\n   302→- **Next.js Guide**: [Next.js Documentation](https://nextjs.org/docs)\\n   303→- **TypeScript**: [TypeScript Handbook](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/)\\n   304→\\n   305→## Recognition\\n   306→\\n   307→Contributors will be:\\n   308→- Listed in README.md\\n   309→- Mentioned in release notes\\n   310→- Invited to contributor Discord channel\\n   311→- Eligible for swag (future)\\n   312→\\n   313→## License\\n   314→\\n   315→By contributing to Efflux Web, you agree that your contributions will be licensed under the MIT License.\\n   316→\\n   317→---\\n   318→\\n   319→Thank you for helping make Efflux Web better! 🚀\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it looks malicious. If it does, you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer high-level questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"93d7d589-9d89-40e9-a53a-324d95550029\",\"uuid\":\"25ac1f55-5a87-438f-9341-4cc370f2f57a\"}"}}
